## Targeting covariates for European hake
Clustering of the catch composition data was conducted by applying the CLARA clustering technique. To obtain the input data matrix for the cluster analysis, the $CPUE_{i,j}$ matrix of record $i$ and species $j$ is first decomposed into its Principal Components (PCs) using Principal Component Analysis (PCA). This is down-weight the influence of the abundance signal of the species of interest and thereby minimising issues with non-independence between the response and the predictor variable for targeting.
For this purpose, the data matrix comprising the $CPUE{i,j}$ records for all reported species was extracted from the dataset. The CPUE records were normalized into relative proportions by weight to eliminate the influence of catch volume, fourth-root transformed and PCA-transformed.
```{r}
# Replace all NA values in the dataframe with 0 for all columns
datf[is.na(datf)] <- 0
# Create dataset with species columns
spd =  datf[,which(names(datf)%in%sp.select)]
# create data for PCA input
pca_dat <- sqrt(sqrt(spd/apply(spd,1,sum)))
pca_dat [is.na(pca_dat)] <- 0
#run PCA
pca<-prcomp(pca_dat, scale=T)
# Predict PCs for each observations
pr_pca <- predict(pca,pca_dat)
pcs <- data.frame(pr_pca[,1:(ncol(pr_pca))])
# Replace all NA values in the dataframe with 0 for all columns
datf[is.na(datf)] <- 0
# Create dataset with species columns
spd =  datf[,which(names(datf)%in%sp.select)]
# create data for PCA input
pca_dat <- sqrt(sqrt(spd/apply(spd,1,sum)))
pca_dat [is.na(pca_dat)] <- 0
#run PCA
pca<-prcomp(pca_dat, scale=T)
# Predict PCs for each observations
pr_pca <- predict(pca,pca_dat)
pcs <- data.frame(pr_pca[,1:(ncol(pr_pca))])
computeOC <- function(eig){
nk <- length(eig)
k <- 1:nk
aparallel <- rep(1, length(eig))
cond1 <- TRUE; cond2 <- TRUE
i <- 0; slopes = NULL
pred.eig <- af <- rep(NA, nk)
while ((cond1 == TRUE) && (cond2 == TRUE) && (i < nk)) {
if(i>0)
slopes = rbind(slopes,data.frame(PCs=1:nk,eig=predict(vp.p,data.frame(ind=1:nk)),PCst=i+1))
i <- i+1
ind <- k[c(i + 1, nk)]
vp.p <- lm(eig[c(i + 1, nk)] ~ ind)
vp.prec <- pred.eig[i] <- sum(c(1, i) * coef(vp.p))
cond1 <- (eig[i] >= vp.prec)
cond2 <- (eig[i] >= aparallel[i])
nc <- i - 1}
out = list(npc = nc,
ncl = nc+1,
eig=eig,
slopes= slopes)
return(out)
}
The input to the function `computeOC` are the eigenvalues of the Principle Components
```{r}
# get Eigenvalue
eig = summary(pca)$sdev^2
# run Optimal Coordinates test
OCtest = computeOC(eig)
# Define numbers of PCs
npc = OCtest$npc
# Define numbers of Cluster as npc+1
ncl = OCtest$ncl
# Clustering with CLARA
cl_sel = clara(pca_dat, ncl,sample=100,sampsize=250,pamLike = TRUE)
# Clustering with CLARA
cl_sel = clara(pca_dat, ncl,sample=100,sampsize=250,pamLike = TRUE)
pcs$FT = as.matrix(cl_sel$cluster)[,1]
pcs$FT = as.matrix(cl_sel$cluster)[,1]
pca_dat$FT = factor(pcs$FT)
# get Eigenvalue
eig = summary(pca)$sdev^2
# run Optimal Coordinates test
OCtest = computeOC(eig)
# Define numbers of PCs
npc = OCtest$npc
# Define numbers of Cluster as npc+1
ncl = OCtest$ncl
# Clustering with CLARA
cl_sel = clara(pca_dat, ncl,sample=100,sampsize=250,pamLike = TRUE)
The results suggest that only the first PC1 is non-trivial and correspondingly two clusters were selected as optimal for the CLARA clustering (Fig. 3) Eso dice en el caso del ejemplo, pero aqui yo diria que los 3 podrían ser importantes aunque no sé muy bien.
```{r fig3, fig.height=7,fig.width=8, fig.cap = paste0("Figure 3: Graphical representation of the three clusters that characterise the different fishing tactics projected over the first two Principal Components (PCs), where only PC1 was determined to be non-trivial.")}
